warrants, over the closed set spec 3 declares
  A warrant states what stands behind a document, and promotion is the act that
  moves one from `asserted` to `accepted`. The set is closed, so every value has a
  row whether a document of this corpus states it or not.
      1 accepted
      0 regenerated   the engine reads this off the generated-file marker
      0 transcribed   the engine reads this off the generated-file marker
      0 asserted      no document of this corpus states it
  Stated on 2 of 5, and absent on 3. Spec 3 makes the value required, so the
  second figure is a population and never a default. The census reported 0 of these rows
  as generated, and the engine reads the warrant of a generated document off the marker
  rather than out of a declaration.
  Values the closed set does not hold, each with the count that states it. No
  check of this engine reads a warrant value, so this is the only line that reports one:
        1 `pending`
  The `asserted` population is 0, which is the denominator a promotion rate divides
  by. The numerator is a count per change, which `headwater check --change` reports under
  `warrant.promoted`: this verb reads one working tree, so it holds one half of that rate
  and can never reach the other. Nothing declares how many promotions in one change is too
  many, so no figure here carries a verdict.

